  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Prove that the composition of functions is associative. That is, show that $(f\\circ g)\\circ h=f\\circ(g\\circ h)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Both compositions have the same domain and codomain and send every $x$ to $f(g(h(x)))$. Hence $(f\\circ g)\\circ h=f\\circ(g\\circ h)$.",
      "worked": "Let $h:X\\to Y$, $g:Y\\to Z$ and $f:Z\\to W$. Both compositions map $X$ to $W$. For every $x\\in X$,\n$$\\begin{align*}((f\\circ g)\\circ h)(x)&=(f\\circ g)(h(x))\\\\((f\\circ g)\\circ h)(x)&=f(g(h(x)))\\\\((f\\circ g)\\circ h)(x)&=f((g\\circ h)(x))\\\\((f\\circ g)\\circ h)(x)&=(f\\circ(g\\circ h))(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThe compositions therefore agree at every input and have the same domain and codomain. Hence $(f\\circ g)\\circ h=f\\circ(g\\circ h)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
